USERNAME=your-name
TEAM=your-team
API_KEY=your-api-key-from-registration
# CLIENT_ID is auto-generated on first run and appended to your .env. It pins
# usage data to a specific machine (PK on the server is username+date+model+client_id+source).
# DO NOT delete, overwrite, or hand-edit it — and if you re-clone the repo, copy
# your existing CLIENT_ID across. A new id looks like a new machine to the server,
# so the old id's rows linger and every overlapping (date, model) gets double-counted
# on your profile. If this happens, contact an admin to re-key the stale rows.
# CLIENT_ID=
# --- Tools, Projects & Communities ---
# Listing what you actually use day-to-day helps the developer community cut through
# the hype. Everyone's tweeting about the latest hot tool, but most of it is vaporware.
# Your real-world usage data shows what's actually being used in production.
#
# TOOLS      — What AI coding tools do you actually use daily? (e.g. superpowers,paperclip,cmux)
# PROJECTS   — What are you spending tokens on? (e.g. tkmx,plow.co)
# COMMUNITIES — What developer communities are you part of? (e.g. bloomberg-ai-engineering,agentcribs-community)
#
# RUNNING ON MORE THAN ONE MACHINE? These profile fields (and ABOUT, HN_USERNAME,
# DEMO_VIDEO_URL below) describe YOU, not the machine — but they're read from the
# .env of whichever machine happens to be reporting. A field left blank is omitted
# from the report entirely — that machine simply sends nothing about it. Set them
# on ONE machine, leave them blank on the rest, and every machine still reports
# its usage. Each report ECHOES the fields it published ("Published from this
# machine's .env — these OVERWRITE your shared profile"), so if a value you
# corrected elsewhere keeps coming back, the machine republishing it says so in
# its own log — blank the field there.
# (For these fields, a variable exported in your shell wins over a blank line here
# — USERNAME is the exception, which is always read from this file. What the
# server does with a value you DO send varies by field: the three badge lists
# below are additive, so editing or blanking a line here never removes an entry
# the server already has. Removing one is a separate command, not a setting:
#   npm run untag -- --list                 show what the server has stored
#   npm run untag -- tools "Typo Name"      remove one badge
# Delete the badge from EVERY machine's .env first. Removal only clears the
# server's copy — any machine still listing it re-adds it on its next report,
# and that report is usually on a timer. Avoid "/" in a badge name: it can be
# created but not removed by that command.
# See "Adding a badge is easy; removing one is a command" in README.md.)
TOOLS=
PROJECTS=
COMMUNITIES=
# About — the main content of your profile page. This is your chance to share what
# you're building and how. Describe your AI coding setup, link to the tools you use,
# and share any content you've created about your workflow:
#   - Blog posts about your setup
#   - YouTube videos or tweets about your tools
#   - Links to the tools/plugins you're using (e.g. arsenal, superpowers)
#   - Your coding philosophy or approach
# URLs are auto-linked on your profile. Example:
# ABOUT="Building with https://github.com/nickarail/arsenal — here's my setup video: https://youtube.com/..."
ABOUT=
# Your Hacker News username (e.g. Sam_Odio). To appear on the Builder Index, add
# your Builder Index profile URL — https://aiworthusing.com/builder-index/u/YOUR_NAME
# — to your HN about section. For a live example, see
# https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=Sam_Odio. An older watchmepivot.com or
# tkmx.odio.dev profile URL already in your about section still verifies; leave it
# as-is. Then click "Verify" on your Builder Index profile.
HN_USERNAME=
# 3-minute demo video (YouTube URL) — embedded on your profile page under the
# "3-MIN DEMO VIDEO" heading. The point is to deliver one aha moment: how AI
# changed a real part of your workflow. Structure it as old world → new world.
#   Old world: one concrete task you used to do before AI — the friction, the
#              steps, how long it took, why it sucked. Keep this short.
#   New world: the same task today. Show the prompt, the agent's output, the
#              shipped result. The viewer should leave thinking "I want that."
# The 3-minute cap is a social contract, not a technical check — longer videos
# get skipped, because nobody owes a stranger more than that. Keep it tight.
# Example: DEMO_VIDEO_URL=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ
DEMO_VIDEO_URL=
# A picture for your profile, intended to replace the generated letter avatar.
# NOT ACTIVE YET: the client reports this, but the profile page does not render
# it yet, so setting it has no visible effect until the server side ships.
# Three ways to say it — pick whichever you already have:
#   AVATAR=https://example.com/me.png   an image you host yourself (https only)
#   AVATAR=gravatar:you@example.com     your Gravatar
#   AVATAR=github:yourhandle            your GitHub profile picture
# The Gravatar form hashes your address locally and sends only the resulting
# URL, so your email never leaves this machine. Square images look best — it's
# rendered as a circle. Leave unset to keep the generated letter avatar.
# Note: the server does not store an avatar yet — it accepts the field and
# ignores it, and no avatar comes back when you read your profile. Setting,
# changing, or blanking this has no visible effect until server support lands.
# (The untag command above is for the badge lists only; it does not apply here.)
AVATAR=
# Number of days of history to report (default: 28). Set to 1 for steady-state after initial sync.
# REPORT_DAYS=1
# Report machine config (hostname, OS, CPU, memory, installed skills, codex version,
# MCP server names, hook events, CLAUDE.md size, shell/terminal/editor, git worktrees).
# This is static setup info — what tools you have installed, not how you use them.
# Shown on your profile so others can see what top devs actually run.
# We NEVER send prompts, responses, conversation history, file contents, or API keys.
# Skills come from three sources, merged: installed plugins
# (~/.claude/plugins/installed_plugins.json), personal skills (~/.claude/skills/*/,
# only directories containing a SKILL.md), and MCP server names (from an mcpServers
# block, or derived from mcp__<server>__* permission entries — server names only,
# never tool names, arguments, or credentials).
# Skills that came from a plugin marketplace also report a canonical public URL,
# so the profile can render them as links; personal skills and MCP servers have
# no upstream and stay unlinked rather than pointing somewhere wrong. Only https
# URLs with a public hostname are sent — a locally-installed marketplace's
# file:// path or git@host: remote is dropped, never published.
REPORT_MACHINE_CONFIG=true
# Keep specific skills or MCP servers off your profile. Comma-separated,
# case-insensitive, dropping a name whichever source produced it.
# Unlike TOOLS/PROJECTS (which are additive server-side), a machine's skill list
# is replaced wholesale on each report, so excluding a name does remove one that
# was already published.
# Note: your profile shows the union across ALL your machines, so an entry only
# disappears once every machine that reports it has excluded it too.
# SKILLS_EXCLUDE=warp,clerk-setup
# Report dev stats — HOW you code, not just what you have installed.
# This is the behavioral data that teaches others how top devs spend 10B+ tokens/week:
#   - Workflow: tool-call frequencies (Bash/Read/Edit/Grep/Agent), session count, avg
#     session length, agent dispatch %, hour-of-day activity, cache reuse ratio
#   - Outcomes: git commits, LOC added/removed, files changed, active repos
#     (from `git log` in repos Claude touched — no repo names or file names sent)
#   - Codex: session count, avg tokens/session, avg session duration
#   - Cursor: AI code attribution (tab-complete vs composer vs human lines)
# Privacy: NO file paths, NO prompt content, NO tool arguments, NO repo names, NO code.
# Only tool names, aggregate counts, and environment strings leave your machine.
REPORT_DEV_STATS=true
# Aggregate Claude Code usage from additional machines without installing the
# client on each. Comma-separated list of directories; each must contain a
# `projects/` subdirectory of JSONL session files (i.e. the same layout as
# ~/.claude). Point this at a synced snapshot of a remote machine's ~/.claude
# and its usage will be collected alongside local usage on every report.
# Example: EXTRA_CLAUDE_CONFIGS=/path/to/synced-laptop,/path/to/synced-desktop
# EXTRA_CLAUDE_CONFIGS=
# Aggregate Codex usage from additional Codex homes beyond the local ~/.codex.
# Comma-separated list of directories; each must contain a `sessions/`
# subdirectory (i.e. the same layout as ~/.codex). Use this to fold in Codex
# accounts whose data lives outside the default scan — e.g. a reviewer bot's
# per-account homes. Usage sums into the codex source alongside the local scan.
# Example: EXTRA_CODEX_CONFIGS=/srv/bot/codex-account-a,/srv/bot/codex-account-b
# EXTRA_CODEX_CONFIGS=
# Aggregate Pi harness usage from additional data directories beyond the local
# AgentsView scan. Comma-separated list of directories; each path is passed
# directly to AgentsView as a Pi data root. Usage sums into the pi source.
# Example: EXTRA_PI_CONFIGS=/srv/pi-data-a,/srv/pi-data-b
# EXTRA_PI_CONFIGS=
# Aggregate OpenCode usage from additional data directories beyond the local
# AgentsView scan. Comma-separated list of directories; each path is passed
# directly to AgentsView as an OpenCode data root. Usage sums into the opencode
# source.
# Example: EXTRA_OPENCODE_CONFIGS=/srv/opencode-data-a,/srv/opencode-data-b
# EXTRA_OPENCODE_CONFIGS=
# OpenAI Admin API key (sk-admin-...) — enables collecting platform.openai.com
# usage via the /v1/organization/usage endpoint. Create at:
#   https://platform.openai.com/settings/organization/admin-keys
# This is a READ-only usage query; the key never appears in the usage report
# we send to the server. Leave unset to skip OpenAI platform collection.
# WARNING: If your Codex CLI is authenticated with an API key (not ChatGPT
# subscription auth), its usage already shows up in platform.openai.com usage
# and setting this will double-count those tokens. Only enable this if Codex
# is using ChatGPT auth or if you don't use Codex CLI.
# OPENAI_ADMIN_KEY=sk-admin-...
# Override OpenClaw auto-discovery with a comma-separated list of session
# directories. Defaults to auto-discovery of standalone (~/.openclaw/...) +
# every macOS Plow variant (co.plow.app, .dev, .wt1, ...). Set this only if
# OpenClaw lives in a non-default location or you want to scope reporting.
# Example: OPENCLAW_SESSIONS_DIRS=/custom/path/agents/main/sessions,/another/path/agents/main/sessions
# OPENCLAW_SESSIONS_DIRS=

# agentsview auto-update — the reporter keeps agentsview current by running
# `agentsview update --yes` at most once per day (best-effort; a failure never
# affects the report). This is a plain binary download+replace, so it does NOT
# hit the macOS launchd sqlite-sync deadlock. Staying current avoids silently
# falling behind the server's minimum agentsview version. Set to false to
# disable and manage agentsview updates yourself.
# AGENTSVIEW_AUTO_UPDATE=false
